WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.680
A deadly camping trip, a green dude's&nbsp;
big break, and a super hero movie that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.300
left everyone in tears. 2022’s the year&nbsp;
for sci-fi movies that’ll make you laugh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.170
cry, and question your existence all at once.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.740
"Prey," the latest installment in the&nbsp;
"Predator" franchise, goes back to 1719,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.120
when one of the first Predators comes to&nbsp;
Earth. This hunter is pitted against Naru,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.360
a Comanche woman looking to prove she&nbsp;
has what it takes to be a great hunter.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.280
"Prey" has breathtaking vistas, bloody&nbsp;
action, and excellent performances. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.840
tease in the credits and Easter eggs from other&nbsp;
"Predator" films will please franchise fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.980
but the biggest reason to see this sci-fi&nbsp;
gem is definitely Amber Midthunder's Naru,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.909
who uses her wits and training against&nbsp;
an alien foe far larger than she.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.800
"Beyond the Infinite Two Minutes" is a&nbsp;
delight from start to finish. Kato lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.820
upstairs from the café he owns, and one&nbsp;
evening, he discovers a time anomaly while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.180
looking at his computer monitor, allowing&nbsp;
him to see two minutes into the future.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.380
Once his friends find out, they want to see if&nbsp;
they can expand the anomaly to take advantage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:03.240
the ensuing foreknowledge, and things spiral from&nbsp;
there. The recursive nature of the repeating two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.680
minutes leads to some philosophical debates,&nbsp;
but "Beyond the Infinite Two Minutes" never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.180
gets too heavy. It's light and breezy — even&nbsp;
when danger looms — and a whole lot of fun.

00:01:12.818 --> 00:01:16.778
"Show them who we are."

00:01:16.894 --> 00:01:20.520
The sequel to "Black Panther," and&nbsp;
the 30th movie overall in the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.320
introduces a second incredibly powerful&nbsp;
and hidden nation to the MCU with Talokan.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:30.360
Talokan is the cinematic update of Atlantis,&nbsp;
home of the submariner Namor who doesn't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.380
the outside world to discover his homeland&nbsp;
for fear that imperial powers will attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.540
and steal their resources. But things aren't that&nbsp;
simple — Namor also demands the life of the young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.100
scientist who unintentionally allowed one of&nbsp;
Talokan's vibranium deposits to be discovered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.620
leading his country into conflict with Wakanda.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:52.440
"Black Panther: Wakanda Forever" is a major-scale&nbsp;
movie even for the planet-hopping franchise. Its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:56.700
international conflict creates a plot that feels&nbsp;
like a political thriller with global stakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.500
and its action scenes are some of the most&nbsp;
exciting we've ever seen in the MCU. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.940
the main thrust of the movie deals with honoring&nbsp;
the legacy of the late Chadwick Boseman and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:10.765
character, King T'Challa, and by the end of the&nbsp;
film, there isn't a dry eye in the audience.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.720
"Something in the Dirt" has a deceptively&nbsp;
small scale for its big ideas. The movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.500
centers on two neighbors who meet shortly after&nbsp;
one of them moves into an apartment building.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.880
They quickly develop a friendship, and when&nbsp;
strange things start happening in the newly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:28.380
leased apartment — like things floating&nbsp;
inexplicably and wild light refractions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:32.880
that create patterns on the wall — they decide&nbsp;
to make a documentary about the phenomena. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.320
as they continue to explore what may be causing&nbsp;
these strange occurrences, they begin to split in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.820
their theories and the methods they want to employ&nbsp;
until we're left with more questions than answers.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:47.220
"Something in the Dirt" is a fascinating movie not&nbsp;
only for the questions it poses in its plot but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:51.960
also because it's a strange mix of mockumentary,&nbsp;
home-video collage, and straightforward narrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.440
that makes it one of the most formally&nbsp;
inventive science fiction movies of the year.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:03:00.600
Maika Monroe is a genre darling for a reason,&nbsp;
and this year she's offered us not one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.940
but two great new genre movies. "Watcher"&nbsp;
delivered a skin-crawling story of a stalker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.820
and "Significant Other" brings&nbsp;
us a high-concept sci-fi movie.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:12.780
"Significant Other" follows couple Ruth&nbsp;
and Harry on a backpacking trip in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.240
Pacific Northwest where they discover a series of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:18.120
strange and disturbing occurrences&nbsp;
in the wake of a comet's arrival.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.800
The movie doesn't offer answers for a while and&nbsp;
instead keeps the audience guessing in an equally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.840
fun and unnerving game of "what's really going on&nbsp;
here?" It also helps that Monroe and costar Jake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.980
Lacy are giving incredibly charming performances&nbsp;
and that the forest setting is beautiful.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.640
The "Dragon Ball" franchise&nbsp;
has always been about aliens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:40.080
as the central Saiyan characters came to&nbsp;
Earth from a different world. But "Dragon Ball&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.520
Super: Super Hero" is one of the most science&nbsp;
fiction heavy movies in the franchise as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.140
focuses not only on aliens but also androids&nbsp;
and a genetically altered "bio-android."

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:53.460
The plot follows the often sidelined&nbsp;
character Piccolo as he takes center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:56.220
stage in investigating the&nbsp;
resurgent Red Ribbon Army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:01.140
a potential threat to the franchise's ensemble&nbsp;
and the world. Like all "Dragon Ball" media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.340
the movie includes some incredibly exciting&nbsp;
and visually stunning animated fight sequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:10.029
but its focus on Piccolo allows for a new&nbsp;
character to be the emotional center of the film.

00:04:10.029 --> 00:04:14.640
From the creative minds that brought us "Free&nbsp;
Guy," "The Adam Project" follows the titular Adam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.080
a time-traveling pilot from 2050 who&nbsp;
gets stranded in 2022 while pursuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:23.580
an unauthorized mission. There, he meets his&nbsp;
12-year-old self, and the two Adams must work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:28.500
together to accomplish older Adam's objective:&nbsp;
Get back to 2018 to save his wife's life.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:33.240
But time travel has a way of making things&nbsp;
complicated — especially since older Adam is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.300
fleeing a dystopian future. With&nbsp;
the odds stacked against them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.120
Big Adam and Little Adam decide&nbsp;
their only solution is to find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.960
man who actually created time travel&nbsp;
itself ... their dearly departed dad.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.920
"The Adam Project" makes maximum&nbsp;
use of Ryan Reynolds' likability as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.460
older Adam. He plays excellently off&nbsp;
his younger self's vulnerabilities;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.280
newcomer Walker Scobell kills his role.

00:04:53.841 --> 00:04:59.901
"God, it's like I traded my brain&nbsp;
for those muscles. It's a s—t deal."

00:04:59.901 --> 00:05:02.340
Plus, there are time jets, and both Reynolds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:06.240
Mark Ruffalo. Who doesn't want to see&nbsp;
Deadpool interact with Bruce Banner?

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:12.060
"After Yang" is a deeply moving exploration of&nbsp;
memory and grief. In an idyllic-looking future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.780
parents Jake and Kyra have provided&nbsp;
their adopted daughter Mika with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:19.920
android sibling named Yang. When Yang&nbsp;
stops functioning, Jake promises Mika&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.380
he will have her "brother" back&nbsp;
to normal as soon as possible.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:27.120
But as they go through Yang's memory bank,&nbsp;
Jake and Kyra make several major discoveries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.160
about Yang's past and the true depths of his&nbsp;
soul. What emerges is breathtaking, emotional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:37.453
and profound — proving filmmaker Kogonada is&nbsp;
one of the very best directors working today.

00:05:37.453 --> 00:05:41.820
Writer-director Richard Linklater&nbsp;
mixes fact and fantasy in "Apollo 10 ½:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:45.600
A Space Age Childhood." A kid&nbsp;
growing up in 1960s Houston,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:49.860
young Stanley is fired up by Apollo&nbsp;
11. As he watches history unfold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:54.240
the real details of the mission are interwoven&nbsp;
with his elaborate dream of being recruited as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.140
an astronaut, because he's small enough to&nbsp;
fit into a lunar module's tight quarters.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:03.060
"Apollo 10 ½: A Space Age Childhood"&nbsp;
embraces nostalgia without completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:06.600
ignoring the upheaval in America&nbsp;
at this time: Houston is just as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:11.400
fascinating as the moon. The movie uses&nbsp;
rotoscoping, a unique animation technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:16.053
to capture Stanley's imagination just as&nbsp;
vividly as the actual launch of the Saturn V.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:20.880
Lonely teenager Suzu leads another life&nbsp;
in the virtual world known as "U." There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.540
she's a famous singer named Belle with&nbsp;
charming freckles and flowing pink hair.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:28.620
After a concert is violently ruined by&nbsp;
a mysterious user known as the Dragon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:32.400
Belle sets out to find him. She soon&nbsp;
discovers they both have tragedy in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.160
their real lives — tragedy they need to overcome.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:38.400
"Belle" features stunning&nbsp;
visuals and real emotional heft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.411
rooted in the trials and&nbsp;
tribulations of adolescence.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:46.740
Sam Raimi infuses the Marvel Cinematic Universe&nbsp;
with his trademark horror in "Doctor Strange in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:51.900
the Multiverse of Madness." Superhero sorcerer&nbsp;
Stephen Strange must protect America Chavez,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:55.080
a dimension-hopping teenager, from&nbsp;
someone seeking to steal her powers.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:59.280
Their flight across the multiverse takes&nbsp;
them to the very limits of existence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:03.480
with Strange grappling with his own issues&nbsp;
and growing his own powers by confronting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:07.680
dark and disturbing versions of himself&nbsp;
across dimensions — both alive and dead.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.420
Plus, Wanda Maximoff emerges as the emotional&nbsp;
core of the film as she struggles with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:16.200
harrowing aftermath of "WandaVision."&nbsp;
What results is a blend of terror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:19.047
fantasy, and sci-fi you won't soon forget.

00:07:19.047 --> 00:07:23.220
Simply put, "Everything Everywhere All&nbsp;
At Once" is one of the best films of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:27.900
year. An endlessly inventive and absurdist&nbsp;
delight, it stars Michelle Yeoh as Evelyn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:33.120
a middle-aged laundromat owner whose IRS audit&nbsp;
becomes a surreal adventure. The multiverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.440
is threatened by a fearsome being, and only&nbsp;
Evelyn can save every dimension everywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:42.240
An eye-popping journey through alternate&nbsp;
universes ensues. Multiple viewings are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.380
must to catch every detail packed into this&nbsp;
wild, heartfelt, and colorful good time.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:49.620
Audiences are told upfront that "Lightyear" is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:52.380
film that made "Toy Story's"&nbsp;
Andy a Buzz Lightyear fan.

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:55.728
"To infinity…" "And beyond."

00:07:55.728 --> 00:08:00.300
We see the titular hero's headstrong insistence&nbsp;
on adventuring solo strand a ship full of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:05.700
fellow Space Rangers on a hostile planet. Buzz&nbsp;
winds up working with a crew of lovable misfits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:10.680
including his former partner's granddaughter and&nbsp;
an adorable robotic cat named Sox, to make things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:15.780
right. But can they overcome Zurg, the enigmatic&nbsp;
villain sending waves of robots after them?

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:20.880
"Lightyear" is a gorgeously animated sci-fi&nbsp;
adventure with lots of comedy and heart. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:24.660
at its best when spotlighting the crew's&nbsp;
attempts to figure out their strengths and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:28.620
when indulging in absolutely incredible&nbsp;
hyperspace sequences. Whether you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:33.887
watching it on an IMAX screen or Disney+, this&nbsp;
Pixar tale is sure to entertain and inspire.

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:39.420
"Neptune Frost" is a singular work of&nbsp;
Afrofuturism. It's a sci-fi musical with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:45.420
an audacious vision that mesmerizes even if, at&nbsp;
times, the story seems elusive. Set in Burundi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:50.220
the film follows a coltan miner named Matalusa&nbsp;
and an intersex runaway named Neptune.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:55.020
At turns impressionistic and poetic, "Neptune&nbsp;
Frost" features characters with symbolic names&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:59.280
such as Memory and Psychology, and it&nbsp;
primarily concerns an anti-colonialist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:03.720
hacker collective waging technowar against&nbsp;
an oppressive police state seemingly run by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:08.640
The Authority. When Matalusa and Neptune meet,&nbsp;
it creates a jolt that powers the collective's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:14.106
technological fight. Be prepared for the visuals&nbsp;
and songs to get stuck in your cerebral cortex.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:17.460
Horror auteur Jordan Peele succeeds once more with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:21.060
"Nope," a sci-fi-horror film about&nbsp;
siblings Emerald and OJ Haywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:25.380
horse wranglers to the stars. When a UFO&nbsp;
begins hovering above their ranch, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:29.580
decide to catch it on tape — but they're not the&nbsp;
only ones with plans for the mysterious entity.

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:35.280
Reporters, photographers, and a sleazy former&nbsp;
child star all have their own designs. "Nope"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.420
tackles society's exploitation of tragedy,&nbsp;
humanity's relationship with animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.680
and the treatment of below-the-line crew&nbsp;
on film sets. This makes for a layered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:47.520
experience that will have you questioning your&nbsp;
own assumptions from the very edge of your seat.

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:53.160
This surreal sci-fi-rom-com adventure stars&nbsp;
Kentucker Audley as James Preble, a government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:58.920
agent who audits people's dreams. That's right: In&nbsp;
the future this movie imagines, dreams are taxed.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:03.900
He soon discovers something more sinister afoot&nbsp;
as he audits Bella, an elderly artist and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:09.540
sole occupant of the titular home. Full of bizarre&nbsp;
logic, oddball characters, and striking imagery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:13.740
"Strawberry Mansion" is a splendid head-trip&nbsp;
guaranteed to expand your imagination.

